aw walrus  unless you're at the women's march or  something  so she's convinced by the internet and  by society that if your child is is  identifying as something then he really  is that thing and so she tries to raise  her child as a walrus as a sort of trans  walrus i knew it would have an impact i  didn't expect i didn't realize  the extent of the impact  that it would have you don't know  anything about this book trying to do  the laws it doesn't sound like a bad  kind of book you know it sounds like a  nice little children's book but  it's not but just the other day someone  came up to me and they said uh there's  obviously someone who completed their  transition to be a walrus  i could tell just by the way they looked  and they told me how much it meant to  them and i said you know i said i and i  thanked her and i said uh well i really  appreciate it and  also i love your music luzo you know how  many fortune 500 companies are run  by trans walruses  zero  how many trans walruses  are in elected office very few  how many trans walruses are in hollywood  michael moore hasn't made a film in  years so  there's certainly a lack of  representation which i which i traced  back to bias which is why i wrote the  book  well i first learned about the trans  residential community when i when i made  it up in my head  it was an incredible moment of  self-discovery  for me when loudoun county said well you  can't speak here unless you're a  resident  and that's my first  all of a sudden kind of a huge  coincidence i heard the voice in my head  that said you're a virginian i lived in  tennessee i felt sort of like a  virginian  trapped in a tennessean's body i  identify as sort of state fluid i guess  i wanted to do something  and i was being told i couldn't do it  and then i realized that it'd be a lot  more convenient if i had this identity  that's when that's when the  confrontation happened  self-identity  can be  can be convenient and it can be  politically useful  an